# Public symbol documentation

Every **exported** function, type, variable, constant, and method must have a godoc comment directly above it.

## Format

- Start with the symbol name: `// FunctionName ...`
- **At most 3 lines** of description.
- **Prefer 2 lines** — one for purpose, one for non-obvious behavior or constraints.
- No filler ("This function…", "Handles the…"); state *what* and *when* it matters.

## Third-party resources

When code calls an external HTTP API, SDK, or tool, the doc **must** include a link to the official docs.

```go
// ✅ GOOD — starts with symbol name, states what + constraint + external link
// ExecVHS runs the VHS CLI against the given .tape file.
// Returns a non-nil error if VHS exits non-zero; see https://github.com/charmbracelet/vhs
func ExecVHS(tapePath string) error { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — short, two lines, no third party
// NewUUUIDAsString returns a deterministic UUID string derived from the input value.
// Same input always produces the same UUID.
func NewUUUIDAsString(input string) string { ... }

// ❌ BAD — no godoc on exported function
func CreateOutputDirectory() error { ... }

// ❌ BAD — comment doesn't start with symbol name; essay style
// This method is responsible for listing all GIF files in the output directory.
// It uses os.ReadDir and filters by .gif extension in various cases...
func ListGIFs() ([]os.DirEntry, error) { ... }
```

## Scope

- **Required:** exported functions, types (`struct`, `interface`, `type alias`), methods, package-level `var`/`const` blocks.
- **Optional:** unexported helpers, file-local functions, one-liner route closures.
- Do not document obvious getters/setters unless the behavior is non-obvious.
